# =============================================================================
# Bault — nightly backup, for the `deploy` user's crontab on the production host.
#
#   sudo install -d -m 700 -o deploy -g deploy /var/backups/bault /var/log/bault
#   sudo -u deploy crontab infra/ops/crontab.example      # REPLACES that user's crontab
#
# /etc/bault/backup.env (mode 600, owned by deploy) holds what the job needs,
# and nothing else:
#
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL='postgres://bault_owner:<pw>@<host>:<port>/<db>?sslmode=verify-full&sslrootcert=/etc/bault/db-ca.crt'
#
# QUOTE that URL. The job below sources this file with `.`, and an unquoted `&`
# ends the assignment there: the shell backgrounds it, DIRECT_DATABASE_URL is
# never set, and every nightly dump fails. (deploy.sh reads the file literally
# and drops the quotes, so the same file serves both.)
#   BACKUP_DIR=/var/backups/bault
#   BACKUP_RETAIN_DAYS=14
#   BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1...            # the PUBLIC key; the private one is not on this host
#   BACKUP_UPLOAD=rclone:backup:<backup-bucket>/nightly
#   RCLONE_CONFIG_BACKUP_TYPE=s3            # the remote `backup`, defined here, no rclone.conf
#   RCLONE_CONFIG_BACKUP_PROVIDER=Other     # AWS on AWS
#   RCLONE_CONFIG_BACKUP_ENDPOINT=https://<region>.your-objectstorage.com   # omit on AWS
#   RCLONE_CONFIG_BACKUP_REGION=<region>
#   RCLONE_CONFIG_BACKUP_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...  # a key for the backup bucket only, PutObject only
#   RCLONE_CONFIG_BACKUP_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
#
# rclone is what infra/production/bootstrap.sh installs. `BACKUP_UPLOAD=s3://…`
# with AWS_* variables and BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT also works, but needs the aws cli,
# which Ubuntu 24.04 does not package; without the tool the job fails before
# it dumps anything.
#
# The backup bucket is in a different account from the database and from the
# image bucket, with versioning (or object lock) on and a lifecycle rule for
# retention, so this host's key can add backups and cannot remove them.
#
# 01:17 UTC: off the hour, and before the worker's 02:00 storage-fee and 03:00
# interest runs, so the dump is the day's state before the nightly money jobs
# touch it. pg_dump reads one consistent snapshot and takes no locks that block
# the API.
#
# A failed run must reach a person. The job's own output goes to the log, so
# cron would have nothing to mail; the `|| echo` prints one line only when the
# dump fails, and cron mails that line to MAILTO (the host must be able to send
# mail). Better still, the commented variant pings a dead-man's-switch monitor
# ONLY on success, so silence (a run that failed, or never started because the
# host was down) is what raises the alarm.
# =============================================================================
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
MAILTO=ops@example.com

17 1 * * * cd /opt/bault && set -a && . /etc/bault/backup.env && set +a && ./infra/ops/backup.sh dump >> /var/log/bault/backup.log 2>&1 || echo "Bault nightly backup FAILED on $(hostname); see /var/log/bault/backup.log"
# 17 1 * * * cd /opt/bault && set -a && . /etc/bault/backup.env && set +a && ./infra/ops/backup.sh dump >> /var/log/bault/backup.log 2>&1 && curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 https://hc-ping.com/<check-uuid> > /dev/null
